From 436129eab318273b2993529c5cad1d677b469631 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zixuan Zhang <zixuan.zhang.uj@renesas.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] drm/bridge: adv7511: stop the KMS poll from tearing the
 pinned link down

With the boot logo hand-over the HDMI output intermittently dies a few
seconds after the compositor comes up: the sink reports no signal, Weston
is still running and the kernel logs nothing at all - the whole failure is
I2C traffic and one uevent.

The path is:

  - "arm64: dts: renesas: rzg3l-smarc: declare the boot logo display
    hand-over" drops the ADV7535 interrupt and "drm/bridge: adv7511: pin
    the connector to the bootloader's mode" advertises
    DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DETECT without DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD, so
    drm_bridge_connector_init() sets

        connector->polled = DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_CONNECT |
                            DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_DISCONNECT

    and rzg2l_du_modeset_init()'s drm_kms_helper_poll_init() calls
    adv7511_detect() every ten seconds.

  - The guard added by "drm/bridge: adv7511: keep an HDMI output
    configured by the bootloader" is one-shot: boot_logo_handover is
    cleared by the compositor's first commit, so from then on the polls
    reach the real detect path.

  - There they hit the "cable was re-plugged" branch.  All three of its
    conditions hold by construction: the ADV7535 has hpd_override_enable
    so ADV7511_REG_STATUS always reports connected, probe forced
    powered = true, and ADV7511_REG_INT(0) still holds the HPD edge the
    bootloader latched when it powered the encoder up.

  - The branch calls adv7511_power_on(), which for a DSI part ends in
    adv7511_dsi_config_timing_gen().  That builds the ADV7535 internal
    timing generator from adv->curr_mode - and curr_mode is all zeroes,
    because the hand-over skips adv7511_mode_set() and nothing else ever
    writes it.  Registers 0x28..0x37 are programmed with a 0x0 mode and
    the generator is reset, so the HDMI output stops.  For good measure
    the branch also returns connector_status_disconnected, which raises a
    hotplug event and makes the compositor drop the output.

It is intermittent because adv7511_hpd() consumes the latch as it reads
it, so only the first poll after the hand-over can trigger this, and
whether the latch is set by then depends on how the sink drives HPD while
it locks onto the bootloader's signal.

Fix it in three independent places:

  - Record adj_mode in curr_mode even when the register writes are
    skipped, so that any adv7511_power_on() reprograms the timing the
    hardware already has instead of zeroes.

  - Keep answering detect() from the pinned state for as long as
    boot_logo_vic is set, not just for the one-shot hand-over window.  A
    connector that is pinned to one mode and connected by construction
    has nothing to learn from the hardware, and "pin the connector to the
    bootloader's mode" already documents this flag as not one-shot.

  - Clear the stale HPD edge in probe, so the re-plug branch cannot be
    entered even if something reaches detect() by another route.

Each of the three is enough on its own; together they also cover the
ordinary DPMS and suspend/resume paths, where a power_on() with an empty
curr_mode would have been just as fatal.  Behaviour without
"renesas,seamless-boot-logo" is unchanged.
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [RZ/G3L fast-boot logo, board specific]
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/adv7511/adv7511_drv.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/adv7511/adv7511_drv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/adv7511/adv7511_drv.c
index 17a5a4967d15..76f24b2ff715 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/adv7511/adv7511_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/adv7511/adv7511_drv.c
@@ -682,11 +682,20 @@ adv7511_detect(struct adv7511 *adv7511, struct drm_connector *connector)
 	int ret;
 
 	/*
-	 * The sink is displaying the bootloader's splash, so it is connected by
-	 * construction.  Answering from the hardware would risk taking the
-	 * "cable was re-plugged" branch below, which re-powers the encoder.
+	 * While the hand-over is pending the sink is displaying the
+	 * bootloader's splash, so it is connected by construction.
+	 *
+	 * Once boot_logo_vic has pinned the connector to a single mode the same
+	 * stays true for the lifetime of the system, and asking the hardware
+	 * can only produce an answer we are not allowed to act on.  Dropping
+	 * the ADV7535 interrupt and DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD put this connector on the
+	 * ten second KMS poll, and each poll would otherwise run the "cable was
+	 * re-plugged" branch below - the pending HPD edge the bootloader left
+	 * latched plus the powered flag set in probe are enough to enter it.
+	 * That re-powers the encoder and reports the sink as disconnected,
+	 * which drops the compositor's output.
 	 */
-	if (adv7511->boot_logo_handover) {
+	if (adv7511->boot_logo_handover || adv7511->boot_logo_vic) {
 		adv7511->status = connector_status_connected;
 		return adv7511->status;
 	}
@@ -978,9 +987,18 @@ static void adv7511_bridge_mode_set(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	 * Keep the timing the bootloader programmed.  It is the same mode, and
 	 * rewriting the timing registers - in particular resetting the DSI
 	 * internal timing generator - makes the sink renegotiate.
+	 *
+	 * Record the mode anyway.  adv7511_dsi_config_timing_gen() builds the
+	 * ADV7535 internal timing generator out of adv->curr_mode, and
+	 * curr_mode has no other writer, so skipping this leaves it at the
+	 * all-zero value devm_kzalloc() gave it.  Any later adv7511_power_on()
+	 * would then program a 0x0 mode into the timing generator and take the
+	 * HDMI output down for good.
 	 */
-	if (adv->boot_logo_handover)
+	if (adv->boot_logo_handover) {
+		drm_mode_copy(&adv->curr_mode, adj_mode);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	adv7511_mode_set(adv, mode, adj_mode);
 }
@@ -1432,11 +1450,20 @@ static int adv7511_probe(struct i2c_client *i2c)
 	 * blank the bootloader's splash.  Record that the device is already up
 	 * instead; the first bridge disable powers it off through the normal
 	 * path.
+	 *
+	 * Clear the HPD edge the bootloader latched in ADV7511_REG_INT(0) while
+	 * it powered the encoder up and drove the HPD override.  adv7511_hpd()
+	 * reads that latch, and combined with powered == true it is exactly
+	 * what makes adv7511_detect() decide the cable was re-plugged.
 	 */
-	if (adv7511->boot_logo_handover)
+	if (adv7511->boot_logo_handover) {
+		regmap_write(adv7511->regmap, ADV7511_REG_INT(0),
+			     ADV7511_INT0_HPD);
 		adv7511->powered = true;
-	else
+		adv7511->status = connector_status_connected;
+	} else {
 		adv7511_power_off(adv7511);
+	}
 
 	i2c_set_clientdata(i2c, adv7511);
 
